{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And you shall make the court of the Mishkan, for the south side southward.</b> When he came to explain the length of the court, he needed to put the south first, to inform us that the hangings of the south stretched a hundred cubits, and likewise the hangings of the north opposite it. And so he did with the Mishkan: when he came to explain its length, he put the south first, and said ״עשרים קרש לפאת נגבה תימנה״ — twenty planks for the south side southward — and after it the north opposite it. And he explains here that the court of the Mishkan had a length of a hundred cubits and a width of fifty, and those outer fifty of its length are none other than the court that was in front of the Mishkan. And the Mishkan was placed within the inner fifty of its length, for the entrance of the Mishkan, which was on the east, was set at the edge of the inner fifty, and the length of the Mishkan concluded at thirty cubits by its measure, so that there were twenty cubits of clearance between the curtains at the back of the Mishkan and the hangings of the court on the west side. And the width of the Mishkan, which was ten cubits, when it was placed within the width of the court, which is fifty cubits, you find that there were twenty cubits of clearance to the north and twenty to the south. You learn from all this that the length of the Mishkan was set within the length of the court, inside those inner fifty, and the Mishkan had clearance on every side: twenty cubits behind it to the west, and twenty to the north and twenty to the south, and a court of fifty by fifty over against the Mishkan. If so, it was a necessary matter to put the south first and after it the north, in order to teach you about its length; and when he came to teach about its width he needed to mention east and west. And the reason he put west before east is because the three directions were covered with hangings — south, north, and west — but the east was not entirely covered with hangings, since the gate of the court was in the middle, as it says: ״ולשער החצר מסך עשרים אמה״ — and for the gate of the court a screen of twenty cubits. And this is what is written: ״לפאת קדמה מזרחה חמשים אמה״ — for the front side eastward fifty cubits — he did not say ״קלעים חמשים אמה״ (hangings of fifty cubits), as he said ״לפאת ים קלעים חמשים אמה״ — for the seaward side hangings of fifty cubits — because the east had a width of fifty cubits, but not all fifty of its width were covered with hangings; rather, fifteen cubits on this side and fifteen on that side, and the gate of the court in the middle, which is twenty cubits. If so, when he mentioned here the four directions in this order and mentioned east last, he mentioned this only according to the order of the covering of the hangings, not according to the directions — for their order of directions is thus: east, west, north, and south, and so they are mentioned by everyone in this order, because the sun begins to shine from the east and sets in the west, and when a person faces east, his right is to the south and his left to the north and his back to the west. And so we find their order in Scripture (Job 23:8–9): ״הן קדם אהלוך ואיננו ואחר ולא אבין לו, שמאל בעשותו ולא אחז יעטוף ימין וגו׳״ — Behold, I go forward but He is not there, and backward but I cannot perceive Him; on the left hand when He acts I cannot grasp Him, the right hand He veils and…" "<b>And the reason</b> he put north before south — though it would have been proper to put the south first, since it is the right side — is because of these three directions: most of them are settled, but the south has no settlement except a little, because of the abundance of heat, since the sun dwells at the height of its direction; therefore he mentioned the south last. And you learn from this that the order of the directions in this passage — south and north and east and west — is according to the order of the covering of the hangings; and their order — east and west, north and south — is according to the order of settlement in the lower world; and the true order above, in the upper dwelling, is south and north and west and east, according to the order of the upper Chariot. And I have already mentioned this above at the verse ״והיו שמונה קרשים״. And these hangings were of fine twisted linen (שש משזר), and they were like a net made of hole upon hole, and they served as walls for the court all around."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:05:07.467726760Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}